 - The game reads the scale when it sets its views' sizes, at start and when the level changes, not every frame. The setting takes effect at start, as the launcher sets it; written into the running game (`DM_CTL`) it has no effect until the level changes. The 3840x2160 output is set at start too.
 - Measured on an M5 Max (40-core GPU, 64 GB, 2026-11-12, docked, a window, [`bench.tsv`](bench.tsv) `kokiri-deck` from the deck save, two runs of each): the main passes at 1920x1080 with 1.0, 2560x1440 with 1.333, 2880x1620 with 1.5 and 3840x2160 with 2.0 (pass dumps in play). 1.0: 56.7 and 60.0 fps, GPU p50 13.41 and 13.85 ms; 1.333: 60.0 fps both, 13.90 and 13.88 ms; 2.0: 60.0 and 59.0 fps, 14.54 and 14.71 ms. So native 3840x2160 costs about 0.7 ms of GPU time a frame over 2560x1440 here, and the game holds 60 fps; the frame's cost on Apple GPUs hardly depends on its resolution ([Frame rate](#frame-rate)). Not measured: a slower Mac, handheld (the output stubs scale the handheld size too; not run), the iPhone.
 
+## Grass and ground cover
+
+`DM_FOLIAGE=off` (the Mac settings' Grass and ground cover row, on by default) turns the game's foliage module off at its source: grass, ferns, clover, the ivy on walls and the moss fuzz on logs. The stub in [`patches.py`](patches.py) (`foliage`, placed by the fingerprint of the module's per-frame calc, `main+0x2c00a40` on v131072) sets the module's own grass and forest disables (+0x141 / +0x142, which its timelines use) and the four per-type hide bits of its debug word (+0x2b1) every frame while state page +0x300 is 1, so the game's own checks skip the foliage draws, the per-type calc and the FoliageShader compute. Cuttable grass and bushes are actors drawn as models and stay. Brad Norman measured it on TVPort (brad-n/TVPort 8609a1cb, MIT) on a Mac mini M2 Pro: the foliage encoders came to about 3.5 ms of GPU a frame (balcony, Link's house) and 0.02 ms with the option, and graphics memory fell about 85 MB (Link's house 1346 → 1256 MB). The state offset differs from TVPort's (+0xb0 there), because upstream's wide-screen stubs use +0xa0 / +0xa4.
+
 ## Memory
 
 - Current, Mac: from about half of Metal's working set the GPU driver frees at every command buffer and the game runs at about 10 fps (Hyrule Field after 15 minutes on a 24 GB M4 Pro, 9.0-9.5 GB required): [the driver's low-water mark](../../../docs/reference/memory.md#the-gpu-driver-frees-at-every-command-buffer-from-about-half-the-working-set). The guard lets go of what has idled from 7.3 GB there and tells the player when the game is slow past 8.4 GB; saving and starting again empties it.
